  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...A shift in the long term\nweather pattern will bring slightly cooler and wetter weather \nfor the end of the week. A weak upper level trough will move \ninto the Pacific Northwest late Wednesday, shifting the high \npressure that brought hot and dry conditions earlier in the week\nto the east by Thursday. Monsoonal moisture will advect into \nthe region from the south, with increased chances of showers and\nthunderstorms beginning early Thursday and peaking on Friday \nand Saturday. High terrain near the Nevada border and over the \nCentral Idaho Mountains will see the highest potential for \naccumulating precipitation, with a 40-60% chance currently \nforecast on Friday and Saturday. Heavy rainfall in these regions\nwill bring a flash flood threat, especially over steep and \nrocky terrain and recent burn scars. Cooler temperatures will \noccur from Thursday through Sunday, with highs about 4-6 degrees\nbelow normal through the weekend. Moisture will taper off late \nSunday into Monday, with a ridge building back in over the \nIntermountain West, bringing a return to warm and mostly dry \nconditions for early next week.",
